Step 1. Identify the Major Mission Phases

The mission description involves two major phases:
• Collection Phase – The robot navigates to storage locations, sends a message with the resource specification, waits for the storage to retrieve the resource, and—if needed—handles a low battery (10%) by returning to the recharging station and reassigning the mission.
• Delivery Phase – After collection, the robot must deliver the resources to a specified location. If the battery level drops to 30% during delivery, the robot must return the resource to a checkpoint and reassign the remaining task. If even that fails, an alert must be triggered.

Also noted is that when multiple resources are needed from different storages, several robots can work in parallel to reduce overall mission time.

──────────────────────────────
Step 2. Define the Top-Level Goal and Its Children

We define a top-level goal:
• G1: Mission Completion  
 – This goal is of type Achieve because it represents the target state in which all resources have been collected and delivered successfully.
 – It has three sub-goals:
  ◦ G2: Resource Collection (Perform Goal)  
  ◦ G3: Resource Delivery (Achieve Goal with fallback for low battery handling)  
  ◦ G4: Battery Monitoring (Query Goal)  

──────────────────────────────
Step 3. Decompose the Sub-Goals into Tasks

For each sub-goal, we list the required tasks:

• For G2 (Resource Collection):
 – AT1: Navigate to Storage  
 – AT2: Request Resource  
 – AT3: Wait for Retrieval  
 – AT4: Battery Low Handling (Collection Phase)

• For G3 (Resource Delivery):
 – In the normal case:  
